(N.D.A.C. 75-20-01.2-01(29) )

In order to be eligible for TANF, a dependent child must be " living in the home of a specified relative ." A child must reside with an individual related by blood (including half-blood), marriage, or adoption who is within the 5th degree of relationship.

Note: Subsidized adoptive children cannot be included in the TANF benefit until the adoption decree is final, unless the child is related within the 5th degree to the caretaker. Once the adoption decree is final, the adoptive child must be included in the TANF benefit.

A dependent child must reside with one of the following individuals :

A natural or adoptive parent (1st degree);

A stepparent if the natural or adoptive parent is not in the home (1st degree);

Stepbrother or stepsister, if the stepparent, natural or adoptive parent is not in the home (2nd degree);

Sibling (including a half sibling) if age 16 or older (2nd degree);

Natural and step grandparent, including great, great-great, or great-great-great grandparent (2nd 3rd, 4th, 5th degree);

Uncle or aunt including a great or great-great aunt or uncle (3rd, 4th, 5th degree);

Nephew or niece including great or great-great niece or nephew (3rd, 4th, 5th degree);

First cousin (an aunt or uncle's child) or first cousin once removed which is an aunt or uncle's grandchild (4th and 5th degree);

A second cousin (a great aunt or uncle’s child) (5th degree);

A spouse of any of the above individuals even after the marriage is terminated by death or divorce.

(Revised 6/1/10 ML #3218)

